  "Number": 2,
  "Question": "An equivalent of the expression\n\n\\[ \\begin{aligned} &\\left(\\frac{x^2+1}{x}\\right) \\left(\\frac{y^2+1}{y}\\right)\\\\ &\\quad+ \\left(\\frac{x^2-1}{y}\\right) \\left(\\frac{y^2-1}{x}\\right),\\\\ &\\qquad xy\\ne0, \\end{aligned} \\]\n\nis:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(1\\)",
    "B": "\\(2xy\\)",
    "C": "\\(2x^2y^2+2\\)",
    "D": "\\(2xy+\\dfrac{2}{xy}\\)",
    "E": "\\(\\dfrac{2x}{y}+\\dfrac{2y}{x}\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "D",
  "Solution": "Both products have denominator \\(xy\\), so combining them first makes the cancellation in their numerators visible. Since \\(xy\\ne0\\), the expression equals\n\\[\n\\frac{(x^2+1)(y^2+1)+(x^2-1)(y^2-1)}{xy}.\n\\]\nExpanding the numerator gives\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&(x^2y^2+x^2+y^2+1)\\\\\n&\\quad +(x^2y^2-x^2-y^2+1)\\\\\n&=2x^2y^2+2.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThe \\(x^2\\) and \\(y^2\\) terms cancel. Therefore the original expression is\n\\[\n\\frac{2x^2y^2+2}{xy}\n=2xy+\\frac{2}{xy}.\n\\]\nThis is choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{D}}\\).",
